    "rawResponseText": "As of **August 2026**, an owner prioritizing affiliation flexibility for a European conversion should consider two distinct groups: **true soft-brand franchises** and **lighter membership/affiliation platforms**.\n\n## Priority shortlist\n\n| Brand/collection | Positioning | Why consider it | Flexibility caveat |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **Handwritten Collection – Accor** | Midscale/upscale boutique | One of the clearest conversion-oriented propositions in Europe: flexible standards, operational flexibility, preservation of the property’s identity and access to Accor’s distribution and ALL loyalty platform. ([group.accor.com](https://group.accor.com/en/news-stories/handwritten-collection-50-hotels?utm_source=openai)) | Confirm required guest-experience elements and technology migration costs. |\n| **Radisson Individuals** | Upper-midscale to upper-upscale | Specifically designed for independent hotels and regional groups to retain their identity. Its expanded Premier, Boutique and Retreats variants improve fit across different asset types. ([radissonhotels.com](https://www.radissonhotels.com/en-us/corporate/media/press-releases/rhg-launches-new-brand-radisson-individuals?utm_source=openai)) | Quality, safety, systems and guest-review thresholds still apply. |\n| **Tapestry Collection / Curio Collection – Hilton** | Tapestry: upscale; Curio: upper-upscale/luxury | Flexible conversion frameworks with strong Hilton distribution, technology and Honors participation. Tapestry is generally the more accessible conversion option; Curio suits more distinctive, higher-rated assets. ([stories.hilton.com](https://stories.hilton.com/releases/hiltons-curio-and-tapestry-collections-celebrate-200th-hotel-milestones?utm_source=openai)) | Hilton’s system and loyalty requirements may make these less “light touch” than affiliation-only alternatives. |\n| **Tribute Portfolio / Autograph Collection – Marriott** | Tribute: upscale/upper-upscale; Autograph: upper-upscale/luxury | Strong Bonvoy and global sales proposition while supporting independent hotel identities. Tribute usually deserves the first look when flexibility matters more than luxury positioning. ([hotel-development.marriott.com](https://www.hotel-development.marriott.com/brands/premium-brands?utm_source=openai)) | Expect a potentially more substantial property-improvement plan and detailed brand/guest-experience assessment. |\n| **Vignette Collection – IHG** | Luxury/lifestyle | Built for independent luxury and lifestyle conversions, with an emphasis on protecting character and limiting unnecessary upfront conversion costs. ([ihgplc.com](https://www.ihgplc.com/en/news-and-media/news-releases/2025/ihg-hotels-and-resorts-conversion-friendly-brands-fuel-growth-opportunities-for-owners?utm_source=openai)) | Selective positioning; likely less suitable for conventional upscale hotels without a strong story. |\n| **The Unbound Collection by Hyatt** | Upper-upscale/luxury | Allows distinctive hotels to maintain individuality while accessing Hyatt and World of Hyatt; already established in European city and resort markets. ([hyatt.com](https://www.hyatt.com/development/ourbrands/theunboundcollectionbyhyatt?utm_source=openai)) | Highly selective and generally best for landmark or experience-led properties. |\n| **BW Signature Collection / BW Premier Collection / WorldHotels** | Midscale through luxury | Worth considering where operating autonomy and conversion economics outweigh the need for the largest global loyalty engine. BWH explicitly positions its collections around independently operated, distinctive hotels. ([development.bwhhotels.com](https://development.bwhhotels.com/content/developers/en_US/portfolio.html?utm_source=openai)) | Commercial strength and contract structure can differ by European country or regional BWH affiliate. |\n| **Trademark Collection by Wyndham** | Upper-midscale and above | A straightforward soft-brand proposition allowing owners to retain their concept, character and independent operating identity. ([development.wyndhamhotels.com](https://development.wyndhamhotels.com/brand/trademark-collection-by-wyndham/?utm_source=openai)) | Assess Wyndham’s actual source-market and loyalty contribution for the specific European location. |\n\n## When maximum independence is the overriding objective\n\nAn owner should also test **membership-style affiliations**, which can impose fewer operating and physical-brand requirements than a conventional hotel franchise:\n\n- **Design Hotels** — particularly strong for distinctive, design-led lifestyle hotels. It uses a non-franchise affiliation model and provides distribution, marketing and Marriott Bonvoy access. ([hotel-development.marriott.com](https://www.hotel-development.marriott.com/ResourceFiles/fdd-document/2025-studiores-fdd-3-31-2025.pdf?utm_source=openai))\n- **Preferred Hotels & Resorts** — designed to provide distribution, sales, loyalty and marketing while protecting the hotel’s independent identity. ([join.preferredhotels.com](https://join.preferredhotels.com/?utm_source=openai))\n- **Small Luxury Hotels of the World** — well suited to small, high-end boutique hotels; its portfolio averages approximately 50 rooms and emphasizes independence. ([slh.com](https://slh.com/about-slh?utm_source=openai))\n- **The Leading Hotels of the World** — appropriate for exceptional five-star assets prepared to meet a selective luxury quality threshold while retaining their own DNA. ([lhw.com](https://www.lhw.com/corporate/about-us?utm_source=openai))\n\nThese affiliations usually provide **greater name, design, F&B and operating freedom**, but may deliver less systemwide demand than Marriott, Hilton, Accor or IHG.\n\n## Special case: regional portfolios\n\nFor an owner converting an **existing regional chain or multi-hotel portfolio**, **Series by Marriott** should be evaluated. It is available in Europe and is expressly intended to let regional brands retain their established name and identity while connecting to Marriott Bonvoy and Marriott’s digital channels. ([hotel-development.marriott.com](https://www.hotel-development.marriott.com/brands/seriesbymarriott?utm_source=openai))\n\n## Practical recommendation by asset type\n\n- **Independent midscale/upscale city hotel:** Handwritten, Radisson Individuals, Tapestry, BW Signature/Premier.\n- **Upper-upscale boutique or resort:** Curio, Tribute, Autograph, Vignette or Unbound.\n- **Design-led luxury hotel:** Design Hotels, Vignette, Autograph, Unbound, SLH or LHW.\n- **Owner seeking the lightest possible affiliation:** Design Hotels, Preferred, SLH or LHW.\n- **Regional chain or portfolio conversion:** Series by Marriott, Radisson Individuals or Handwritten.\n\nThe final comparison should focus not only on headline fees, but also on the **property-improvement plan, mandatory systems, loyalty-program costs, procurement rules, owner naming rights, operator choice, agreement length, area protection, liquidated damages and post-termination system exit costs**. In practice, these provisions determine whether a “soft” brand is genuinely flexible.",
